For every posterior vector, $$\label{fnd:tv-pointwise}
 \frac{|\zeta|}{\sqrt3}
 \leq\left\|p-\operatorname{Unif}(\mathcal S)\right\|_{\mathrm{TV}}
 \leq\frac23|\zeta|.$$ Consequently, writing $\mu_\ell=\mathbb Ex_\ell$ for the depth experiment and $a_\ell$ for its mean total variation advantage, $$\label{fnd:tv-moment}
 \frac{\mu_\ell}{\sqrt3}\leq a_\ell
 \leq\frac23\sqrt{\mu_\ell}.$$ In particular, vanishing advantage is equivalent to vanishing posterior second moment.

Let $Q_\ell$ be the marginal likelihood law of the depth-$\ell$ root experiment. Then $$\label{fnd:law-recursion}
 Q_{\ell+1}=\mathcal R_{\lambda,D}(Q_\ell),\qquad
 Q_0=\frac13\sum_{i\in\mathcal S}\delta_{3\mathbf e_i},$$ where $\mathbf e_i$ is the coordinate unit vector and, for every bounded measurable $f$, $$\label{fnd:operator}
 \int f\,d\mathcal R_{\lambda,D}(Q)
 =\sum_{n\geq0}\mathbb P(D=n)
   \int Z f(m^{\mathrm{out}})\,dQ^{\otimes n}.$$ In this formula Equation <a href="#fnd:product" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="fnd:product">[fnd:product]</a> is applied to $m_{i,j}=1+\lambda(\widetilde m_{i,j}-1)$, where $\widetilde m_{\cdot,j}$ are the independent vectors sampled from $Q$. Each conditional integral has total mass one. The operator preserves balanced laws and permutation symmetry.

Let $s=1$ in the regular model and let $s$ be the survival probability in the Poisson model. For the limiting experiment, $$\label{fnd:survival-moments}
 \mu^2\leq s\nu,\qquad |\eta|\leq\sqrt{\mu\nu}.$$ In the Poisson model with $d>1$, $0<s<1$ and $$\label{fnd:survival-equation}
 -\log(1-s)=ds,\qquad
 s\leq2(1-1/d).$$ In particular, at $d\lambda^2=1$, $s\leq2(1-\lambda^2)$.

Put $$c_0=\frac{37}{27},\qquad q=\frac{c_0}{2+\lambda},\qquad
 c=c_0+2\lambda q.$$ Then $$\label{pos:moment-estimates}
 0\le\mathbb EJ\le\mu-\lambda q\nu,
 \qquad c\nu\le\eta\le\sqrt{\mu\nu}.$$ If $s=1$ in the regular model and $s$ is the survival probability in the Poisson model, then $$\label{pos:moment-region}
 \frac{\mu^2}{s}\le\nu\le\frac{\mu}{c^2},\qquad
 \mu\le\frac{s}{c^2}.$$

In either offspring model, $$\label{pos:H-bound}
 \frac{3H}{\alpha}\ge
 \frac{3\mu^2}{2(1+2\mu/3)}-\frac\lambda{\sqrt2}\mu\nu.$$

In the regular range of Proposition <a href="#wei:exclusion" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="wei:exclusion">16</a>, or in its Poisson range, one can use constants $$(H,U)=\left(\frac1{16},\frac{15}{32}\right)$$ to obtain $$\label{wei:hz}
 h\le\overline h:=1-\frac\xi2-H\xi^2-\frac H2\xi^3,
 \qquad z\ge\underline z:=\xi+U\xi^2.$$ For Poisson offspring the stronger values $(H,U)=(1/12,1/2)$ also hold. Every nontrivial fixed point in the claimed ranges satisfies $.03<\xi<1$.
